<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizwa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nizwa is an oasis city in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajar-mountains">Hajar Mountains</a>. It was the capital of the Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> from the 9th century and long a centre of religious scholarship, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> of the Ya'aruba dynasty built its massive round fort in the 17th century. Its fort and market remain landmarks, and it was again the seat of the imams in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizwa.jpg" alt="Nizwa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andries Oudshoorn · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nizwa_(7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nizwa is the largest city in Ad Dakhiliyah Governorate and lies about 140 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, at the base of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajar-mountains">Hajar Mountains</a>. It acquired its importance as a meeting point amid a spread of date palms, at the crossroads of routes linking the interior with Muscat and the lower reaches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a>. Historians cannot agree on the origin of its name: some derive it from an Arabic verb meaning being alone, others from an old water spring. It is a centre of date growing and the market place of the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition holds that in 630 a handwritten letter from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> asked the people of Nizwa to convert. They sent a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Muhammad sent a tutor to teach them the fundamentals of the religion. The city's Jama, its grand mosque, was long a centre of Islamic learning, and the So'al Mosque dates from the 2nd century AH (9th century CE). In the 14th century the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> described a city at the foot of a mountain, enveloped by orchards and streams, with fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and splendid clean mosques.</p><p>Nizwa Fort was built in 1668 by Imam Sultan Bin Saif Al Ya'rubi. The main bulk took about twelve years to complete, and it stands above an underground stream. It served as the administrative seat of the imams and walis and as a stronghold against raiders. In the early 1950s the fort's large round tower was bombed and rocketed by the British Royal Air Force, called in to help Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur</a> suppress a revolt by leaders of the interior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, a conflict driven by a struggle for a share of the country's newly found oil wealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1970 Nizwa has been modernised, with a road link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, a hospital and educational institutions including the University of Nizwa. The fort, the mosque and the traditional souq stand next to one another in the centre and were renovated in the 1990s with traditional materials. The souq is known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> jewellery and khanjars, the curved daggers made by local craftsmen. Falaj Daris, the largest falaj, or irrigation system, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, was named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006, and palm farms stretch for eight kilometres along the wadis Kalbouh and Al Abiad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nizwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nizwa</b> became the capital and a centre of scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-ibadi-imamate-of-oman">First Ibadi Imamate of Oman</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1913 Ibadi scholars and tribal leaders elected Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi as imam at Tanuf; his forces took <b>Nizwa</b> and threatened Muscat, which was saved by British Indian troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a></li><li><blockquote>The imams built forts and palaces, including the round tower of <b>Nizwa</b> and the palace at Jabrin, and extended falaj irrigation.</blockquote><a href="/state/yaaruba-dynasty">Ya'aruba dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
